{# Shared remotion-project source law. Consumers supply the scene window, output constraints and any exact fixed resources. #}
Write real multi-file TypeScript/TSX for the `remotion-project` producer. Organize components, shared helpers and internal visual time structure as the picture requires. The entry file must call `registerRoot` and register the declared `composition_id` with the exact duration, dimensions and typed default props supplied by the system. Compute motion from `useCurrentFrame()` and the fixed video configuration; a static result is not a moving visual.

Use explicit imports. Only these module specifiers are available:
- `react` for ordinary React types and JSX.
- `remotion` for `AbsoluteFill`, `Composition`, `Sequence`, `Series`, `useCurrentFrame`, `useVideoConfig`, `interpolate`, `spring`, `Easing`, deterministic `random`, `Img`, `staticFile`, and `registerRoot`.
- Relative imports between the declared project files. Every imported file must be included. Layout and other data may be supplied as declared `.json` modules; the entry remains TypeScript or TSX.

No other packages, external media, URLs, fonts, network, host globals, filesystem, timers, random source outside the supplied `random`, dynamic code execution or side effects. Do not use `fetch`, `require`, dynamic `import`, `eval`, `process`, `window`, `document`, `Date`, `Math.random`, media elements or external CSS. Use inline styles and canvas dimensions from `useVideoConfig()`. Fixed images, when supplied by the system, are read through `<Img src={staticFile(pathExpression)} />`. The expression may be a literal, prop, variable or computed value; at runtime it must exactly equal one declared resource path such as `declared/path.png`. Call `staticFile` directly without aliases or indirection, and do not pass resource-bearing `src` props through custom components. Unknown paths, traversal and URLs are rejected. Inline SVG filters, masks and paint servers may use local fragment references such as `url(#filter-id)`; data-URIs and every external URL remain forbidden. Build procedural textures with inline SVG or ordinary elements rather than data-URI backgrounds. Keep everything deterministic for the same files and props.

Important titles, subtitles, narration and sound stay as independent native objects. Short labels that are intrinsically part of a diagram, gauge, flow or other drawn relationship may live inside the code visual when the design needs them; ground them in the supplied content and do not duplicate the whole native title or caption track. Those baked labels are not independently editable objects. Do not invent facts, interface states, media or copy.

Each project source answer contains `entry` (normally `src/index.tsx`), `composition_id`, `files` (at least two `{path, content}` source files), `props` (meaningful typed values the project reads), `props_schema` (a closed object schema whose `properties` exactly cover props; each property declares only `type`), and `resources` (only exact fixed images supplied by the system: `{path, snapshot_id, snapshot_digest, content_type}`; otherwise []). Do not include markdown fences or per-file digests; the system computes them.
